[=#firebirdlimitations]
= Limitations =

The implementation of the Firebird protocol isn't 100% complete.  Some notable limitations follow:

* TLS/SSL encryption is not supported.  Firebird's own wire encryption is supported instead.  See [firebirdprotowirecrypt.html Wire Encryption].
* The Firebird services manager is not supported, so administration tools that use it (eg. fbsvcmgr, and gbak and gsec in service mode) can't be used for backup and restore, user management, database repair or statistics.  They fail with "Feature is not supported" or "Cannot attach to services manager".
* Event notification is not supported.  A client that registers for events is refused.
* Creating or dropping a database (eg. isql's CREATE DATABASE and DROP DATABASE) is not supported, and fails with "Feature is not supported".
* Two-phase commit is not supported.
* Requests sent in Firebird's binary request language (BLR) instead of SQL are only partly supported - enough for isql's SHOW commands.  Others are refused with an error.
* Some rarely used operations are not supported, including pinging the server, scrollable cursors (both only available above the default [firebirdprotoversion.html Protocol Version]), and reconnecting to a limbo transaction.

Except for an unsupported BLR request, a request that runs into one of these limitations ends the client's session, after the client is sent an error.
